3:\usepackage{amssymb, amsmath, xcolor}
53:\title[Chromatic Numbers]{Chromatic Numbers with Open and Nonzero Local Modular Constraints}
65:Sid Richardson Building,
79:\keywords{odd-sum colorings, odd-sum chromatic number}
83:    In this paper, we explore chromatic numbers subject to various local modular constraints. For fixed $n$, we consider proper integer colorings of a graph $G$ for which the closed and open neighborhood sums have nonzero remainders modulo $n$ and provide bounds for the associated chromatic numbers $\chi_n(G)$ and $\chi_{(n)}(G)$, respectively.  In addition, we provide bounds for $\chi_{(n,k)}(G)$, the minimal order of a proper integer coloring of $G$ with open neighborhood sums congruent to $k\mod n$ (when such a coloring exists) as well as precise values for certain families of graphs.
94:\section{Introduction}
100:Graph coloring is one of the most well-studied areas in graph theory. Perhaps the most well-known graph coloring problem is the problem of finding proper colorings of the vertices of a graph $G$. The minimum number of colors in such a coloring is the well-studied chromatic number of $G$, $\chi(G)$, which is, despite its long history, still an object of active research \cite{cambie2024removing, campena2023graph, cervantes2023chromatic, char2023improved, haxell2023large, heckel2021non, isaev2021chromatic, matsumoto2021chromatic}. Many other graph invariants can be defined by considering the minimum number of colors under different constraints.
101:As a variation on the well-studied topic of odd colorings \cite{RemarksOdd, CranstonSparse, Cranston1Planar, KnauerBoundedness, PetrChiPlanar, NeighborhoodParity}, this includes the \emph{odd-sum chromatic number} of a graph $G=(V,E)$, denoted $\chi_{\rm{os}}(G)$, which gives the minimum size of the range of a proper $\Z$-labeling $\ell:V\longrightarrow \Z$ such that at every vertex, the sum of its label along with the labels of the adjacent vertices is odd. This concept has been studied in \cite{CaroYairPetru2023, CranstonOddSum}, which includes general bounds given by Caro, Petru\v{s}evski, and \v{S}krekovski \cite{CaroYairPetru2023} as well as various bounds for certain classes of graphs. This idea has been further generalized in \cite{ClosedNeighborhoodSums} by considering proper colorings where all these neighborhood sums have remainder $k \mod n$ for some fixed integers $n$ and $k$, with associated chromatic number $\chi_{n,k}(G)$. Thus, we have $\chi_{2,1}(G)=\chi_{\rm{os}}(G)$.
103:%One such invariant is the \emph{odd-sum chromatic number} of a graph $G=(V,E)$, denoted $\chi_{\text{os}}(G)$, which denotes the minimum size of the range of a proper $\Z$-labeling $\ell:V\longrightarrow \Z$ such that at every vertex, the sum of of its label along with the labels of the adjacent vertices is odd. This concept is also well-studied \cite{OddSumColorings, RemarksOdd, CranstonOddSum, Cranston1Planar, KnauerBoundedness} with general bounds given by Caro, Petru\v{s}evski, and \v{S}krekovski \cite{NeighborhoodParity} and bounds for certain classes of graphs given by a variety of authors \cite{PetrChiPlanar, CranstonSparse, CaroYairPetru2023}. This idea was generalized in \cite{ClosedNeighborhoodSums} by considering proper colorings where these neighborhood sums are congruent to  $k$ modulo $n$ for fixed integers $k$ and $n$, with associated chromatic number $\chi_n(G)$ ($\chi_{2,1}(G)=\chi_{os}(G)$).
105:In Sections 3 and 4 of this paper, we consider an alternative generalization of this notion. In particular, we note that having remainder $1 \mod 2$ is the same as \emph{not} having remainder $0\mod 2$. Of course, for $n>2$, these ideas are not equivalent and we develop the theory of proper colorings where no neighborhood sum has remainder $0 \mod n$. We provide bounds and specific values for some families of graphs for the associated chromatic number $\chi_n(G)$.
107:In the remainder of the paper, we consider \emph{open neighborhood sums}, i.e., the sums over vertices adjacent, but \emph{not} equal to, a fixed vertex. In Sections 5 and 6, we develop results for $\chi_{(n,k)}(G)$, the minimum size of the range of a proper $\Z$-labeling of a graph $G$ such that all open neighborhood sums have remainder $k\mod n$; and in Sections 7 and~8, we consider $\chi_{(n)}(G)$, the minimum range size of a proper $\Z$-labeling of a graph $G$ with no open neighborhood sum congruent to $0$ modulo $n$.
113:%Given a finite, simple graph $G=(V,E)$ with vertex set $V$ and edge set $E$, let $\ell:V\longrightarrow \Z$ be a \emph{labeling} of the vertices of $G$.  We denote the open neighborhood of $v\in V$ by $N(v)$ and the closed neighborhood of $v$ by $N[v]$. Finally, we denote the \textit{chromatic number} of $G$ by $\chi(G)$, given by the minimum order of a proper labeling of $G$. There are extensive results for bounds on the \emph{chromatic number} of a variety of classes of graphs and variations of $\chi(G)$ seen in the literature (\cite{cervantes2023chromatic},\cite{cambie2024removing}, \cite{haxell2023large}, \cite{campena2023graph}, \cite{isaev2021chromatic}, \cite{char2023improved}, \cite{matsumoto2021chromatic}, and \cite{heckel2021non}).
115:%A related concept that has been introduced \cite{OddSumColorings} and has been extensively researched (see \cite{CranstonOddSum},\cite{KnauerBoundedness},\cite{Cranston1Planar}, and \cite{RemarksOdd}) is that of an \textit{odd-sum} coloring. We say a coloring is an \textit{odd-sum} coloring given that for all $v\in V$, we have $\sum_{w\in N[v]} \ell(w) \equiv 1 \mod 2$ or equivalently $\sum_{w\in N[v]} \ell(w) \not\equiv 0 \mod 2$. Denote this by $\chi_{\text{os}}(G)$. In \cite{NeighborhoodParity} Caro, Petru\v{s}evski, and \v{S}krekovski found a general upper-bound for the odd-sum chromatic number of planar graphs ($\chi_{\text{os}}(G))$, that is when the minimum number of colors required to give a proper coloring of $G$ is an odd-sum coloring. Petr and Portier \cite{PetrChiPlanar} tightened the results in \cite{NeighborhoodParity}, while Cranston \cite{CranstonSparse} sharpened this bound for various types of planar and triangle-free planar graphs. Further work on the odd-sum chromatic number has been done in \cite{CaroYairPetru2023} to obtain the tight upper-bounds for some outerplanar and bipartite graphs as well as various inequalities for general non-empty graphs.
117:%In this work we aim to generalize and expand on the aforementioned results by investigating the chromatic number of three different types of colorings of a graph $G$: the \emph{chromatic number of $G$ with remainder $k \mod n$}, the \emph{chromatic number of $G$ with nonzero remainder $\mod n$}, and the \emph{chromatic number of $G$ with open remainder $k \mod n$}.
119:%We define the \emph{chromatic number of $G$ with remainder $k \mod n$}, written $\chi_{n,k}(G)$, is the minimum order of a proper coloring with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$. Note that $\chi_{\text{os}}(G)$ is equivalently $\chi_{2,1}(G)$. We examine $\chi_{n,k}(G)$ for different classes of graphs, with success in finding a fixed value for $\chi_{n,k}(G)$ for complete graphs, paths, stars, friendship graphs, bipartite graphs, cycles, and cones of graphs , along with additional results on infinite tilings.
121:%In our pursuit of findings for the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with remainder $k$ mod $n$, we expand our research to include a second type of coloring. We consider the coloring of graphs in which no neighborhoods are equal to $0$ mod $n$, call the minimal coloring required to be the \emph{chromatic number of $G$ with nonzero remainder $\mod n$}, and denote this as $\chi_n(G)$. Work done by Nagy and Pach \cite{ChiNDNE} examine a variation of this construction on unicyclic graphs, building upon the work of Sutner from the late 1980's (see \cite{GofEden}).
123:%The last coloring we find of interest in this paper includes the open neighborhoods instead of closed neighborhoods - that is the \textit{chromatic number of $G$ with open remainder $k$} mod $n$, which we denote as $\chi_{(n,k)}(G)$. Some work has been done related to this method (see \cite{FabriciConflictFree}) where tight upper-bounds have been found for outerplanar graphs, although the remainder $k$ mod $n$ variation introduced here has little known results in the literature.
126:%In section 2 we make the formal definitions required, however, given the many findings noted on the odd-sum chromatic number, it is worth the remark here that $\chi_{\text{os}}(G)$ is equivalently $\chi_{2,1}(G)$. Thus, all of our results for $\chi_{n,k}(G)$ may be generalized to the odd-sum chromatic number or other colorings in literature that are equivalent for various values of $k$ and $n$.
128:% The rest of this paper is constructed as follows: we introduce our formal definitions in Section 2. Following this we discuss results on the three types of graph colorings this paper is concerned with: Section 3 is dedicated to the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with remainder $k$ mod $n$, and Section 4 to examples thereof. Section 5 is dedicated to the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with nonzero remainder mod $n$, and Section 6 to examples thereof. In section 7 we have various miscellaneous results and in Section 7.1 we focus on the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with open remainder $k$ mod $n$. Finally, Section 8 is dedicated to concluding remarks, \textcolor{red}{open problems, conjectures, etc.}.
