1411:%     Due to the fact that every open neighborhood must sum to $k$, we must have all the vertices on level $d-1$ labeled $k$, as the open neighborhood of each leaf is a vertex on level $d-1$. \\Now if we look at the neighborhood of a vertex, $v$ at level $d-2$, we must have $2k+c_{d-3}\equiv k \implies c_{d-3}\equiv -k$, where $c_{d-3}$ denotes the color of the neighbor of $v$ on level $d-3$. Continuing this pattern, we get all of the vertices on even levels colored with each level receiving one color, which will be $$(-1)^{\frac{d-x-1}{2}}f(\frac{d+1-x}{2})k$$ where $f(n)$ is the nth number in the Jacobstal sequence $\{0,1,1,3,5,11,\dots \}$, with $f(0)=0$, and $x$ is the level of the vertex.\\
1412:% Now to color the odd-level vertices, we begin by labelling $v_{(1,1)}=0$ and $v_{(1,2)}=k$. Then we will label $v_{(3,1)}=0$, $v_{(3,2)}=k$, $v_{(3,3)}=0$, and $v_{(3,4)}=k$. We also label $v_{(3,5)}=v_{(3,6)}=v_{(3,7)}=v_{(3,8)}=0$. In general, if a vertex is labelled $0$, its "grandchildren" will be labelled $\{0, k, 0, k\}$, and if a vertex is labelled $k$, its "grandchildren" will be labelled $\{0, 0, 0, 0\}$. This always guarantees that open neighborhoods of vertices on even levels sum to $k$mod$n$.\\
