83:    In this paper, we explore chromatic numbers subject to various local modular constraints. For fixed $n$, we consider proper integer colorings of a graph $G$ for which the closed and open neighborhood sums have nonzero remainders modulo $n$ and provide bounds for the associated chromatic numbers $\chi_n(G)$ and $\chi_{(n)}(G)$, respectively.  In addition, we provide bounds for $\chi_{(n,k)}(G)$, the minimal order of a proper integer coloring of $G$ with open neighborhood sums congruent to $k\mod n$ (when such a coloring exists) as well as precise values for certain families of graphs.
119:%We define the \emph{chromatic number of $G$ with remainder $k \mod n$}, written $\chi_{n,k}(G)$, is the minimum order of a proper coloring with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$. Note that $\chi_{\text{os}}(G)$ is equivalently $\chi_{2,1}(G)$. We examine $\chi_{n,k}(G)$ for different classes of graphs, with success in finding a fixed value for $\chi_{n,k}(G)$ for complete graphs, paths, stars, friendship graphs, bipartite graphs, cycles, and cones of graphs , along with additional results on infinite tilings.
128:% The rest of this paper is constructed as follows: we introduce our formal definitions in Section 2. Following this we discuss results on the three types of graph colorings this paper is concerned with: Section 3 is dedicated to the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with remainder $k$ mod $n$, and Section 4 to examples thereof. Section 5 is dedicated to the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with nonzero remainder mod $n$, and Section 6 to examples thereof. In section 7 we have various miscellaneous results and in Section 7.1 we focus on the chromatic number of a graph $G$ with open remainder $k$ mod $n$. Finally, Section 8 is dedicated to concluding remarks, \textcolor{red}{open problems, conjectures, etc.}.
156:    If no proper closed coloring with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$ exists, we say that $\chi_{n,k}(G)$ does not exist.
157:    Otherwise, if proper closed colorings with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$ exist of finite order, the \emph{closed chromatic number of $G$ with remainder $k \mod n$}, written \[\chi_{n,k}(G),\] is the minimum order of a proper closed coloring with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$. If such colorings exist only of infinite order, we write ${\chi_{n,k}(G)=\infty}$.
163:\begin{definition}\label{def: closed coloring with nonzero remainder}
167:    If no proper closed coloring with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $G$ exists, we say that $\chi_{n}(G)$ does not exist.
168:    Otherwise, if proper closed colorings with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $G$ exist of finite order, the \emph{closed chromatic number of $G$ with nonzero remainders $ \mod n$}, written \[\chi_{n}(G),\] is the minimum order of a proper closed coloring with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $G$. If such colorings exist only of infinite order, we write $\chi_{n}(G)=\infty$.
175:\begin{definition}\label{def: open colorings}
180:    If no proper open coloring with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$ exists, we say that $\chi_{(n,k)}(G)$ does not exist.
181:    Otherwise, if proper open colorings with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$ exist of finite order, the \emph{open chromatic number of $G$ with remainder $k \mod n$}, written \[\chi_{(n,k)}(G),\] is the minimum order of a proper open coloring with remainder $k\mod n$ of $G$. If such colorings exist only of infinite order, we write $\chi_{(n,k)}(G)=\infty$.
185:    If no proper open coloring with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $G$ exists, we say that $\chi_{(n)}(G)$ does not exist.
186:    Otherwise, if proper open colorings with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $G$ exist of finite order, the \emph{open chromatic number of $G$ with nonzero remainders $ \mod n$}, written \[\chi_{(n)}(G),\] is the minimum order of a proper open coloring with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $G$. If such colorings exist only of infinite order, we write $\chi_{(n)}(G)=\infty$.
