    We have $\chi_{(n)}(K_{i,j})=2$ if and only if all vertices of $V_1$ can be labeled with the same label $\alpha$ and all vertices of $V_2$ with the same label $\beta$ for distinct $\alpha,\beta \in \Z$ with $i\alpha \not\equiv 0 \mod n$ and $j\beta \not \equiv 0 \mod n$. This is possible if and only if $n\nmid i$ and $n\nmid j$.

    If $n\mid i$ and $n \nmid j$, then a proper open $3$-coloring with nonzero remainders $\mod n$ of $K_{i,j}$ is obtainable by labeling one vertex of $V_1$ with $1$ and the rest with $0$ and all the vertices of $V_2$ with $n+1$. The case where $n\nmid i$ and $n \mid j$ is done similarly.

    If $n\mid i$ and $n\mid j$, then labeling $V_1$ needs at least two colors as does labeling $V_2$. But as these colors must be mutually distinct to get a proper coloring, we are done.
